@dzshzx I opened a broader reliability PR here: https://github.com/dzshzx/codexcomp/pull/6

It includes the work I’ve been testing locally around Codex WebSocket/state handling, safer hidden continuations, better cleanup/cancellation, zero-reasoning retry removal, and metadata-only diagnostics/health checks.

The main goal is to make `codexcomp` more reliable for real interactive Codex sessions, where `previous_response_id`, incremental WS turns, continuation folding, and upstream truncation behavior all interact.

@smileBeda Apologies, I didn’t post specifics earlier because they mostly line up with what others have already reported.

On the candy eval with high reasoning, I get 516 reasoning tokens across 5/5 attempts without the proxy, with 0% accuracy every time. With the proxy, I can push it to around 4,000-5,000 reasoning tokens in up to 4/5 attempts (up to 80% accuracy after several runs, emphasis on up to).

So not claiming this is a fix, but it is clearly better than what I was seeing before. More importantly, I can feel the difference in the consistency of Codex output on my actual tasks, though I haven’t pushed anything past 20 minutes today. Just sharing the experience in case someone is still on the fence, feeling frustrated, and hasn’t tried anything yet.
